WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.958
Some control families no cloud
provider can ever close for you,

2
00:00:03.958 --> 00:00:06.212
and program management is the first.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:09.778
NIST treats the whole family
as organization-wide,

4
00:00:09.778 --> 00:00:14.701
outside any system baseline, and
FedRAMP doesn't assign it to any class.

5
00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:16.590
It comes from FISMA and OMB,

6
00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:21.400
and it binds the agency whether or
not it uses a single cloud service.

7
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:26.288
No tool can write an organization's risk
tolerance or appoint its senior official.

8
00:00:26.288 --> 00:00:29.131
These controls close when
the artifact exists,

9
00:00:29.131 --> 00:00:33.650
a named official has approved it, and
the agency can show it's still followed.

10
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:38.141
Think of the security program plan,
the appointed senior security officer,

11
00:00:38.141 --> 00:00:42.488
a signed risk tolerance, and one
organization-wide monitoring strategy.

12
00:00:42.488 --> 00:00:47.226
One distinction matters to an assessor:
the program inventory lists systems,

13
00:00:47.226 --> 00:00:50.262
and each system's inventory
lists what's inside it.

14
00:00:50.262 --> 00:00:51.538
The two must agree.

15
00:00:51.538 --> 00:00:53.810
Two more families close the same way.
